--- body ---
## Context
Subagents start without project memory. No `profiles/*/PROFILE.md` mentions it, and the instruction to
read `memory/MEMORY.md` at task start lives in `AGENTS.md`, which bare profiles do not load. Injecting every
topic costs about 22K tokens per spawn (86 KB of `memory/MEMORY.md` + `memory/topics/`, estimated at 4 bytes per
token); the index alone is about 2 KB. Author's pick (2026-10-28): option C.

## Design
- Each worker profile (`coder`, `code-reviewer`, `gate-seat`, `prose-reviewer`, `team-lead`) gains one line:
  read the project's `memory/MEMORY.md` at task start and open the topics the brief names or that match the task.
  Missing memory never blocks work (as in AGENTS.md § Project memory).
- The delegation rule (`rules/workflow.md` § Delegation, "Delegate intent, not procedure") adds: a brief names
  the memory topics relevant to the task, as paths, when any apply.
- README: the Subagents component section states the mechanism in one or two sentences, cross-referenced from
  the Memory section (layer 3, Topics). (Author asked "README section 3 subagents"; Subagents is component 7,
  Memory component 3: mention in Subagents, link from Memory.)
- Runtime-agnostic: the mechanism is text in profiles and rules, so Claude Code, Codex and Pi get it alike.

## Exit criteria
- [ ] Every `profiles/*/PROFILE.md` carries the memory-index line; `tests/test_profile_tool_grants.py` still passes (Read is granted).
- [ ] `rules/workflow.md` § Delegation names topics-in-the-brief in one line.
- [ ] README Subagents section describes the mechanism; Memory section links to it.
- [ ] `make check` passes.
